**Missed API:**

1. **java.util.Iterator.hasNext()**: This method is needed to implement the stack's iterator functionality. It needs to be implemented to return true if there are more elements in the stack, and false otherwise. This is necessary to provide a way to iterate over the elements in the stack.

Here's an example implementation of a custom stack using Java's built-in API:

```java
import java.util.Iterator;
import java.util.NoSuchElementException;

public class CustomStack {
    private int[] elements;
    private int size;

    public CustomStack(int initialCapacity) {
        elements = new int[initialCapacity];
        size = 0;
    }

    public void push(int element) {
        if (size == elements.length) {
            resize();
        }
        elements[size++] = element;
    }

    public int pop() {
        if (size == 0) {
            throw new NoSuchElementException("Stack is empty");
        }
        return elements[--size];
    }

    public int peek() {
        if (size == 0) {
            throw new NoSuchElementException("Stack is empty");
        }
        return elements[size - 1];
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return size == 0;
    }

    public int size() {
        return size;
    }

    public Iterator<Integer> iterator() {
        return new Iterator<Integer>() {
            private int index = 0;

            @Override
            public boolean hasNext() {
                return index < size;
            }

            @Override
            public Integer next() {
                if (!hasNext()) {
                    throw new NoSuchElementException("No more elements");
                }
                return elements[index++];
            }
        };
    }

    private void resize() {
        int[] newElements = new int[elements.length * 2];
        System.arraycopy(elements, 0, newElements, 0, elements.length);
        elements = newElements;
    }
}
```

This implementation provides a custom stack with push, pop, peek, isEmpty, and size methods, as well as an iterator that allows you to iterate over the elements in the stack.

